## 2. Algorithm & Machine Learning Approaches

### RG.org Sports Betting Algorithms Guide

**Source**: [Sports Betting Algorithms](https://rg.org/guides/sportsbetting-guides/sports-betting-algorithms)

#### Monte Carlo Simulations (Our Approach!)

**Definition**: Model probabilities by running simulations thousands of times

**Why It Works for NBA**:
- Accounts for inherent uncertainty and variability
- Explores multiple potential outcomes
- Adjusts predictions based on real-time data

**Our Implementation**:
- **2,500 runs** per player/game
- Each run uses Poisson distribution for discrete events
- Aggregates to mean, median, floor (10th percentile), ceiling (90th percentile)

#### Reinforcement Learning

**Concept**: Algorithm learns to optimize predictions by:
- **Rewarding** accurate forecasts
- **Penalizing** incorrect ones
- Continuously improving over time

**Application to Ludi**:
- Track bet results in Module R (Ledger)
- Feed outcomes back to Module C (Oracle)
- Adjust feature weights based on performance
- This is your "RL Agent" brain file (`ludi_brain.pkl`)

#### Machine Learning Data Requirements

**Essential Inputs**:
1. Player statistics (box score + advanced)
2. Team strength metrics
3. Weather conditions (outdoor sports)
4. Historical outcomes
5. **Player fatigue** (rest days, B2B)
6. **Real-time adjustments** (injuries, lineup changes)

**Neural Networks**:
- Analyze relationships between variables
- Team strength × Player fatigue × Weather
- Make real-time adjustments based on in-game developments

